  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1150 AM CST THU NOV 19 2026\n\nA series of disturbances remains situated along the Gulf Coast\nand the east coast of Florida, with low rain chances remaining in\nthe forecast through tomorrow due to increased moisture aloft.\nThis flow of moisture will more or less get \"cut off\" over the\nnext 36-hours, as the flow aloft becomes more organized, pushing\nthese disturbances off to the east. Once this occurs, dry air\nwill quickly filter back into the area, with little to no rain\nexpected through Saturday.\n\nHowever, by Sunday afternoon, a cold front will begin to approach\nfrom the west, as a weak trough begins to work into the Midwest.\nRainfall amounts with this cold front are generally expected to\nbe minimal, and mostly limited to our far NW zones Sunday night\nand into Monday. Rainfall chances will increase once again by\nMidweek, as another system looks to work into the region, but\nconfidence in organized rainfall here is low, as this system\ncurrently doesn't look all that potent. Otherwise, temperatures\nwill remain warming through the weekend, before falling once\nagain come next week.\n\n/44/",
